In your React application, it seems you are setting up routes using `react-router-dom`, but you may not have included your navigation (menu) within the `Layout` component, or you might not be rendering the `Header` and `Footer` correctly within your `Layout`. 

Here are a few steps to address the issue and ensure that the navigation is visible on all routes:

### 1. Update Your Layout Component

Make sure that your `Layout` component includes the navigation and it is rendered properly regardless of which route is active. An example of how you might set up your `Layout` component is shown below:

```jsx
// components/Layout.js
import React from 'react';
import { Layout, Menu } from 'antd';
import { Link } from 'react-router-dom';

const { Header, Content, Footer } = Layout;

const LayoutComponent = ({ children }) => {
    return (
        <Layout>
            <Header>
                <Menu theme="dark" mode="horizontal">
                    <Menu.Item key="home">
                        <Link to="/">Home</Link>
                    </Menu.Item>
                    <Menu.Item key="test">
                        <Link to="/Test">Test</Link>
                    </Menu.Item>
                </Menu>
            </Header>
            <Content style={{ padding: '20px' }}>
                {children}
            </Content>
            <Footer style={{ textAlign: 'center' }}>Footer Content</Footer>
        </Layout>
    );
};

export default LayoutComponent;
```

### 2. Modify Your App Component

In your `App` component, you also need to ensure that the children are passed to your `Layout` component. You can do this by wrapping the `Route` components with the `Layout` and passing children to it as shown below:

```jsx
import logo from './ressources/logo.png';
import './App.css';
import React from 'react'
import { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';
import ExampleComponent from './components/ExampleComponent';
import Layout from "./components/Layout";
import Home from './components/Home'

function App() {
    return (
        <BrowserRouter>
            <Layout>
                <Routes>
                    <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
                    <Route path="/Test" element={<ExampleComponent />} />
                </Routes>
            </Layout>
        </BrowserRouter>
    );
}

export default App;
```

### Summary

1. Make sure your `Menu` and navigation are included in the `Header` of your `Layout` component.
2. Ensure you are passing `children` to the `Layout` component properly, so the content of your routes is rendered correctly within the layout.

With these changes, your navigation should be visible across all routes.

